{"findings": [{"file": "src/ev/loop.zig", "line": 892, "severity": "minor", "category": "correctness", "title": "`.duration` timers armed through `Loop.add` still read a cached `now`, so the backdating window is narrowed, not closed", "body": "The defect the change fixes is that `armTimer` computes a `.duration` deadline from `LoopState.now[0]`, a snapshot taken at some earlier point. The new refresh after a waiting backend poll removes the largest source of staleness (up to `max_wait`, 60s by default), but the snapshot is still arbitrarily old relative to the moment the timer is armed: everything the caller runs between one `poll` returning and the next one starting shares that single snapshot. In this runtime that is a whole executor task batch (`Executor.tick_target_ns` is only a target, and a task that does not yield can overrun it without bound), plus every callback dispatched in `processCompletions`.

The path is real and is the one the new test's own comment names: `Timeout.asyncWait` (src/time.zig:584) arms a select timeout with `executor.loopAdd(&ctx.timer.c)`, which reaches this `.timer` branch and `LoopState.armTimer` (src/ev/loop.zig:415-422). A task that runs 5ms into the batch and then arms a 1ms select timeout gets a deadline already in the past and a timeout that fires at once, which is the same failure class, only smaller.

The asymmetry is visible inside this file: `Loop.setTimer` (src/ev/loop.zig:632-634) calls `updateNow()` before `armTimer` for exactly this reason, and it pays one VDSO `clock_gettime` per arm to do it. `Loop.add` does not. Making the arm site read the clock (or calling `updateNow` in this branch, as `setTimer` does) removes the class instead of shrinking it; the poll-site refresh then becomes an optimisation rather than the correctness mechanism."}, {"file": "src/ev/loop.zig", "line": 980, "severity": "trivial", "category": "docs", "title": "The `checkTimers` comment justifying lock-free `now`/`tick` access still enumerates only two `updateNow` call sites", "body": "The comment reads "`now`/`tick` are only ever touched by the owning executor thread (`updateNow` is called here and in `setTimer`, both owner-thread; the cross-thread `clearTimer` never reads them), so no timer lock is needed." The safety argument is an exhaustive enumeration of the writers, and the change adds a third writer in `poll` (src/ev/loop.zig:1436-1438) that, unlike `setTimer`, does not hold `timer_mutex`. The new site is still owner-thread, so the conclusion holds, but the enumeration a future reader checks against is now wrong, and this comment is the only place the invariant is recorded. Add the `poll` site (and `init`) to the list, or move the argument onto `updateNow` itself as a documented owner-thread-only contract."}]}
